---
// GDPR (EU 2016/679) privacy page. Decided:
//   1. The controller is whoever legally runs the site: M-Squad GmbH (filled in on
//      9 October 2026; name, address and managing director as in the imprint).
//   2. The live build runs Plausible on M-Squad's own instance (ANALYTICS in
//      src/config.ts, configured 12 October 2026). Section 3 and the meta description
//      below say so. If the instance or the tool changes, rewrite both first.
//      Section 3's sentences on clicks and downloads mirror two settings in the
//      instance (Settings > General > Tracking: Outbound links and File downloads
//      ticked, Form submissions unticked because the site has no form). When those
//      ticks change, change the sentences; if a form is ever added, decide on the
//      third. What the instance keeps, and for how long, is the owner's to confirm:
//      README, "Launch requirements".
//      The page states no consent conclusion on purpose. Running without a banner is
//      a position, not a settled fact, and the owner or counsel has not yet accepted
//      it (the README records who and when). The script sets no cookies and writes
//      nothing to the device. It sends the page address and the referrer, scroll
//      depth, engaged time, and the address of clicked outbound links and downloaded
//      files. It also reads one local-storage flag (plausible_ignore, which excludes
//      your own visits) and navigator.webdriver (a bot check); neither is sent. The
//      EDPB (Guidelines 2/2023 v2.0, para 33) says JavaScript that instructs the
//      browser to send information "clearly falls within the scope of Article
//      5(3)"; para 53 treats information that client-side code sends back to a
//      server as a gaining of access; para 56 adds that this does not by itself
//      make consent necessary. § 25 TDDDG has two exceptions in Abs. 2 and none for
//      statistics. The DSK (OH Digitale Dienste v1.2, Nov 2024) makes no statement
//      that reach measurement is consent-free (Rz. 87 to 89), at most for a
//      precisely defined configuration that stops holding when more information is
//      added, and says even simple counting is not automatically part of the basic
//      service (Rz. 90). Plausible's own legal assessment concludes from "does not
//      store any information on the user's device" and does not discuss the script
//      reading the page address or the referrer. This configuration measures more
//      than bare counting. Any added embed, tool or measured data needs this page
//      and that position re-checked before it ships.
// Still open:
//   3. German-market sites: translate this page into German (Datenschutzerklaerung)
//      and serve it at /datenschutz; German law expects legal pages in German.
//   4. A solid baseline, not legal advice. When in doubt, have it reviewed.
// The copy below already passes the a11y / seo / tone / email suites: keep one
// <h1>, the description in the 120-160 char window, no em dashes or banned
// contractions, and every email address behind <EmailLink>.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import EmailLink from '../components/EmailLink.astro';
import { SITE, COMPANY } from '../config';
---
<Base
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} handles personal data: cookieless analytics, hosting on Cloudflare Pages, contact data handling, and your rights under the GDPR.`}>
  <section class="wrap">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p>
      Last updated: 12 October 2026. This page explains which personal data this
      website processes, on what legal basis under the GDPR (EU 2016/679), and
      which rights you have.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Controller</h2>
    <p>Responsible for data processing on this website:</p>
    <p>
      {COMPANY.legalName}<br />
      {COMPANY.street}<br />
      {COMPANY.postcodeCity}<br />
      Managing director: {COMPANY.managingDirector}<br />
      Contact: <EmailLink address={COMPANY.email} subject="Privacy request" />
    </p>

    <h2>2. Hosting and server logs</h2>
    <p>
      This site is served by <strong>Cloudflare Pages</strong> (Cloudflare, Inc.,
      101 Townsend St, San Francisco, CA 94107, USA). When you visit a page,
      Cloudflare processes technical connection data (IP address, date and time,
      requested URL, browser user agent) to deliver the site and to protect it
      against attacks. The legal basis is our legitimate interest in operating a
      secure and fast website (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). Cloudflare acts as our
      processor under Art. 28 GDPR; transfers to the USA are covered by the
      EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework and standard contractual clauses. We do not
      combine these logs with any other data.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Cookies and analytics</h2>
    <p>
      This website is ad-free and <strong>sets no cookies</strong>. Your visit
      leaves no advertising or cross-site tracking identifiers with us.
    </p>
    <p>
      To understand how the site is used, we run <strong>Plausible
      Analytics</strong>, a web analytics tool, on our own server at
      analytics.m-squad.com, operated by {COMPANY.legalName}. Its script sets no
      cookies and writes nothing to your device. It checks one setting in your
      browser, which lets you exclude your own visits, and whether the browser
      is automated; neither is sent to the measurement server.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you open a page, the script sends the address of that page and the
      page or site you came from to that server, together with the technical
      data your browser sends with every request (browser, operating system,
      device type, IP address). When you leave the page or switch to another tab or window, it also sends how far
      you scrolled and how long the page was visible and in focus. It also
      counts clicks on links to other websites and sends the address of the link
      you clicked. It counts clicks on links to files, such as the slide deck,
      and sends the address of the file.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tool derives your approximate location from the IP address and does not keep the IP address in its own records. From your IP address, your browser data
      and this website's domain, plus a value that is replaced every 24 hours,
      it forms an identifier. While that value lasts, and as long as those inputs stay the same, the tool therefore recognises the same browser and counts it as one visitor. Once the value is replaced, it can no longer recognise that browser, and it cannot recognise it on another website. The individual events (for example each page view, and the scroll depth and engaged time sent when you leave a page or switch away from it) and the session records are stored with this identifier, the page address, the referrer, any link or file address sent with the event, the location and the device data, and are shown as aggregated counts, for example page views, referrers and countries. Requests to that server pass
      through Cloudflare, which also serves this site (see section 2).
    </p>
    <p>
      The legal basis is our legitimate interest in understanding how the
      website is used and in improving it (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR).
      The individual events and session records are deleted from the measurement server after 24 months.
      You can prevent the measurement by blocking the analytics script in your
      browser.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Contact by email or phone</h2>
    <p>
      If you contact us by email or phone, we process the data you provide (name,
      email address or phone number, and the content of your message or call) to
      handle the request and possible follow-up questions. The legal basis is
      Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR where the request relates to a contract, otherwise our
      legitimate interest in answering enquiries (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). We keep the
      correspondence, and any note we make of a call, only as long as needed for
      this purpose and for any statutory retention duties.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Under the GDPR you have the right of access (Art. 15), rectification
      (Art. 16), erasure (Art. 17), restriction of processing (Art. 18), data
      portability (Art. 20), and objection to processing based on legitimate
      interest (Art. 21). To exercise any of these rights, contact us via the
      address in section 1; we will verify your identity before acting on a
      request. You also have the right to lodge a complaint with a data
      protection supervisory authority (Art. 77 GDPR).
    </p>

    <h2>6. Changes to this page</h2>
    <p>
      We will update this page whenever the data processing on this site
      changes, and adjust the date at the top accordingly.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
